  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nTODAY...The upper low and associated deep cold frontal zone will \ntraverse the region, with continued scattered to numerous showers \nalong with a few embedded thunderstorms and areas of drizzle, mainly \nthis morning, with precipitation chances gradually exiting to the \neast this afternoon. Still looking at the potential for localized \nmodest to briefly high rainfall rates, especially overnight through \nearly morning over far southeast KS. However, given the limited \ninstability, rainfall rates will likely remain less than one inch \nper hour for most, so a higher-end flash flood threat appears \nunlikely, with any minor flooding likely confined to low-lying and \ntypical flood-prone areas. The current flood watch runs through \nmidday, and we may be able to cancel all or portions of it early. \n\nFor this afternoon, could have renewed scattered shower/storm \ndevelopment over southeast KS in vicinity of the advancing cold \nfront. Despite decent effective shear, severe weather is unlikely \ngiven meager instability and poor lapse rates. \n\nSUNDAY NIGHT--MONDAY...A stubborn southwest CONUS upper low is \nexpected to finally eject east-northeast this weekend, possibly \nbring some low precipitation chances to Mid-America Sunday night \ninto Monday. Lift and moisture is not very impressive with this \nsystem, and the GFS has backed off some, so left the forecast dry \nfor now.\n\nMID-LATE NEXT WEEK...Deterministic and ensemble consensus supports \nanother deep western CONUS upper trough taking shape next week, \nwhich could support another round of showers/thunderstorms across \nthe region by mid to especially late week. Still quite a bit of \nuncertainty and model spread, so stay tuned as we refine forecast \ndetails in the coming days.\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nTODAY...Had to undercut NBM high temperatures once again today due \nto widespread clouds/precipitation and the cooler airmass spreading \nsoutheast across the region. That said, thinking central KS will \nstay in the 50s today, with 60s and 70s further southeast. \nStout/gusty north winds will gradually expand southeast today as the \nsurface pressure gradient tightens in wake of the advancing cold \nfront.\n\nFRIDAY-SATURDAY...A deepening central to eastern North American \nupper trough will drive strong Canadian high pressure south into Mid-\nAmerica, supporting the coolest air thus far this fall season. High \ntemperatures in the 50s to lows 60s appear likely. Additionally, \nstout/gusty north winds Friday will make it feel even cooler. The \ncoolest night will be Friday night, with lows likely dipping into \nthe 30s for most underneath the strong high pressure. Given the \nlight winds and clear skies, frost is looking probable for much of \nthe forecast area with the exception of possibly far southeast KS \nearly Saturday.\n\nSUNDAY THROUGH NEXT WEEK...Model consensus supports a quick return \nto seasonable or even above normal temperatures next week, as \nanother deep upper trough sets up over the western CONUS, with \nbreezy south winds developing once again by Sunday.",
